So on this one we're
going to talk about some

3
00:00:15,281 --> 00:00:20,153
Linux utilities for
IP networking in Linux.

4
00:00:20,153 --> 00:00:25,068
So just stepping back, make sure we're all
on this same page, same understanding,

5
00:00:25,068 --> 00:00:27,682
let's just kind of give
a little background.

6
00:00:27,682 --> 00:00:33,324
So internetworking is one
of the key parts of IP,

7
00:00:33,324 --> 00:00:36,909
it's the Internet protocol.

8
00:00:36,909 --> 00:00:40,763
So this is really about
enabling communication

9
00:00:40,763 --> 00:00:44,433
between multiple heterogeneous networks.

10
00:00:44,433 --> 00:00:49,259
So if you recall at the link
layer like Ethernet or Wi-Fi or

11
00:00:49,259 --> 00:00:53,397
any of those, well,
we're going to have local area

12
00:00:53,397 --> 00:00:58,042
networks that are on various
different technologies.

13
00:00:58,042 --> 00:01:04,190
So IP is the protocol that really
binds all of these together and

14
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:08,253
enables the communication across them.

15
00:01:08,253 --> 00:01:14,050
So the key here is this
view from a paper in 1974

16
00:01:14,050 --> 00:01:19,719
where they introduced
the concept of a gateway.

17
00:01:19,719 --> 00:01:25,438
And this is put a router at the edge of
each network that, [COUGH], can translate

18
00:01:25,438 --> 00:01:31,347
effectively between the boundaries of
the two different networks, all right?

19
00:01:31,347 --> 00:01:35,929
And it was called a gateway in that paper.

20
00:01:35,929 --> 00:01:42,325
So addressing here in IP is as follows.

21
00:01:42,325 --> 00:01:46,708
So each interface gets an IP address.

22
00:01:46,708 --> 00:01:51,114
So shown here we have a diagram
of couple end hosts and

23
00:01:51,114 --> 00:01:54,829
then a couple network elements in between.

24
00:01:54,829 --> 00:02:00,503
And so each of the interfaces would
get an IP address, all right?

25
00:02:00,503 --> 00:02:06,083
IPv4 is kind of what's been
most commonly used for

26
00:02:06,083 --> 00:02:09,985
the past 1020 whatever years.

27
00:02:09,985 --> 00:02:14,177
And it's 32 bits in dotted decimal.

28
00:02:14,177 --> 00:02:19,847
So you'll see an address like this,
192.168.0.1.

29
00:02:19,847 --> 00:02:22,455
So all of that is 32 bits.

30
00:02:22,455 --> 00:02:27,763
Now IPv6 is certainly
gaining a lot of momentum and

31
00:02:27,763 --> 00:02:31,599
becoming pretty ubiquitous by now.

32
00:02:31,599 --> 00:02:35,193
And so
this is just a lot bigger addresses.

33
00:02:35,193 --> 00:02:37,590
So 128 bits, all right?

34
00:02:37,590 --> 00:02:41,859
And then because they're bigger,

35
00:02:41,859 --> 00:02:48,840
they have its own way of kind
of displaying the addresses.

36
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:53,712
So the first Linux utility to know about

37
00:02:53,712 --> 00:02:57,705
is called ip addr or IP address.

38
00:02:57,705 --> 00:03:01,088
You can write out the full
name if you want.

39
00:03:01,088 --> 00:03:07,068
So here, each device,
if it's participating in IP networking

40
00:03:07,068 --> 00:03:13,282
will have at least one address to
use the corresponding protocol.

41
00:03:13,282 --> 00:03:17,705
And it's possible to have several
different addresses attached to one

42
00:03:17,705 --> 00:03:18,300
device.

43
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,521
And when we say device,

44
00:03:20,521 --> 00:03:26,532
these are the things that you can
set up with the IP link utility.

45
00:03:26,532 --> 00:03:31,276
So an ethernet device,
a bridge, GRE tunnel,

46
00:03:31,276 --> 00:03:37,098
all of those different things
are what we call in a device.

47
00:03:37,098 --> 00:03:43,041
So here's the demand page,
you can go look at all the options it has,

48
00:03:43,041 --> 00:03:49,923
but the main way you'll be using it is you
specify ip addr and then some command.

49
00:03:49,923 --> 00:03:53,480
So a command would be add as shown here.

50
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:59,248
But there's also commands to remove,
show, change, all of those.

51
00:03:59,248 --> 00:04:03,448
Then you specify the device,
so dev eth1 that you

52
00:04:03,448 --> 00:04:08,355
want to apply this address to and
then the address, right?

53
00:04:08,355 --> 00:04:09,985
It's pretty simple.

54
00:04:09,985 --> 00:04:11,870
And so that's a command.

55
00:04:11,870 --> 00:04:15,702
Now, the address eth1 has the address or

56
00:04:15,702 --> 00:04:20,638
the device eth1 has the address 10.0.1.2.

57
00:04:20,638 --> 00:04:27,593
If I did ip addr show,
I would be able to see that.

58
00:04:27,593 --> 00:04:33,552
All right, so with that, now let's move on
to really what is the role of a router?

59
00:04:33,552 --> 00:04:41,794
So routing is what computes paths
by coordinating with neighbors.

60
00:04:41,794 --> 00:04:48,216
And then forwarding is the thing that
directs traffic to a destination.

61
00:04:48,216 --> 00:04:53,717
So shown here is a bunch of
networks all connected together.

62
00:04:53,717 --> 00:04:55,913
These are routers that are running.

63
00:04:55,913 --> 00:05:01,620
And, [COUGH], the routing protocol would
communicate between each other to say,

64
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:05,176
here's the path,
here's paths I know about, and

65
00:05:05,176 --> 00:05:09,991
they'll all determine what's
the best path that they should take.

66
00:05:09,991 --> 00:05:13,760
And it's all dependent on what
routing protocol is being run.

67
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:18,967
Then when a host wants to actually
send traffic, it would send it, and

68
00:05:18,967 --> 00:05:24,089
then the forwarding or the data plane
of the router would take what was

69
00:05:24,089 --> 00:05:29,762
pre-computed as those paths and say,
I know how to get to the destination.

70
00:05:29,762 --> 00:05:35,635
So for this particular destination,
I'm going to go through this sequence

71
00:05:35,635 --> 00:05:41,046
of routers and each one only really
has to know what its next hop is, and

72
00:05:41,046 --> 00:05:46,300
then the next one will figure out
how it's forwarding the traffic.

73
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:52,080
All right, so
we talked about the address and

74
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:54,976
how to add it in Linux.

75
00:05:54,976 --> 00:05:59,778
And really there's
a concept called a prefix.

76
00:05:59,778 --> 00:06:08,076
So an IP prefix is of the form a.b.c.d/z.

77
00:06:08,076 --> 00:06:12,737
So we might see something like 2.1.1.0/24.

78
00:06:12,737 --> 00:06:17,664
So that z, or in this case 24,
indicates how many of

79
00:06:17,664 --> 00:06:22,601
the upper order bits uniquely
specifies that group.

80
00:06:22,601 --> 00:06:27,056
So this represents several IP addresses in

81
00:06:27,056 --> 00:06:32,008
the range 2.1.1.0, 2.1.1.1,

82
00:06:32,008 --> 00:06:36,352
2.1.1.2, and so forth, right?

83
00:06:36,352 --> 00:06:39,652
Because it's the upper 24 bits.

84
00:06:39,652 --> 00:06:44,516
If we drew this out, so
each of these numbers

85
00:06:44,516 --> 00:06:49,385
in the dotted decimal
format is eight bits.

86
00:06:49,385 --> 00:06:53,663
So that's why it's 8,
16, 24 represents, and

87
00:06:53,663 --> 00:06:59,618
that's why those first three numbers
in this case is what I was pointing to.

88
00:06:59,618 --> 00:07:06,689
So why this is important in the Internet
is because it adds scalability.

89
00:07:06,689 --> 00:07:12,873
So now instead of having to know all
the addresses on the Internet and

90
00:07:12,873 --> 00:07:18,405
how to get to them, this router
right here just has to know for

91
00:07:18,405 --> 00:07:22,444
anything that's 2.1.1.0/24.

92
00:07:22,444 --> 00:07:27,288
So anything that the first
24 bits has those values,

93
00:07:27,288 --> 00:07:30,035
I'm going to send it out port1.

94
00:07:30,035 --> 00:07:33,426
I didn't have to know about
all of the addresses,

95
00:07:33,426 --> 00:07:36,190
I just need to know, send it out port1.

96
00:07:36,190 --> 00:07:38,927
When it gets closer to the destination,

97
00:07:38,927 --> 00:07:43,754
it may split that out, or probably it
will split it out such that it knows

98
00:07:43,754 --> 00:07:48,358
how to get to each individual
elements at once within that network.

99
00:07:48,358 --> 00:07:51,346
But I didn't need to know that
in the rest of the Internet.

100
00:07:51,346 --> 00:07:55,124
And that's what makes this scalable.

101
00:07:55,124 --> 00:07:57,689
All right, and then with that,

102
00:07:57,689 --> 00:08:02,735
what that means is we have to do
longest prefix matching or LPM.

103
00:08:02,735 --> 00:08:05,133
And this is in each of the routers,

104
00:08:05,133 --> 00:08:10,120
it's finding the most specific
prefix that matches the destination.

105
00:08:10,120 --> 00:08:16,278
So if you recall from a previous
course inside of the router,

106
00:08:16,278 --> 00:08:21,228
we'll have a table,
the forwarding table, and

107
00:08:21,228 --> 00:08:26,441
it will have a bunch of different entries,
right?

108
00:08:26,441 --> 00:08:33,447
So here we have two different entries
where the first 16 bits are the same,

109
00:08:33,447 --> 00:08:37,729
11.11, but the 24 is a longer prefix.

110
00:08:37,729 --> 00:08:39,787
So this is more specific.

111
00:08:39,787 --> 00:08:44,862
So if we see traffic
destined to 11.11.11.11,

112
00:08:44,862 --> 00:08:50,856
it'll actually choose this second
entry and then send it out port2.

113
00:08:50,856 --> 00:08:58,381
If it was just say 11.11.12.12, well,
it would only match this first entry,

114
00:08:58,381 --> 00:09:02,945
this first 16 bits and so
it would send it out port1.

115
00:09:02,945 --> 00:09:05,712
So that's longest prefix matching.

116
00:09:05,712 --> 00:09:10,082
[COUGH], all right, so
final kind of preview or

117
00:09:10,082 --> 00:09:16,419
review slide is talking about
the generic router architecture.

118
00:09:16,419 --> 00:09:19,261
And so this is showing IP forwarding.

119
00:09:19,261 --> 00:09:24,300
So in general,
a packet comes in with a header

120
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:29,483
which includes some
destination IP address.

121
00:09:29,483 --> 00:09:32,970
Inside of the router will
be a forwarding table.

122
00:09:32,970 --> 00:09:36,369
And so this is like what we were
showing on the previous slide.

123
00:09:36,369 --> 00:09:40,704
And so the IP address for that,
that is part of in the header,

124
00:09:40,704 --> 00:09:45,465
the destination will be looked up
using longest prefix matching in

125
00:09:45,465 --> 00:09:49,815
the forwarding table,
which will tell what the next hop is.

126
00:09:49,815 --> 00:09:52,708
Then the header will be updated.

127
00:09:52,708 --> 00:09:58,874
We'll queue the packet up when
storing in some packet buffers and

128
00:09:58,874 --> 00:10:05,725
send it out the specific network
interface as determined by the lookup.

129
00:10:05,725 --> 00:10:08,850
So Linux has all that,
we've talked about that.

130
00:10:08,850 --> 00:10:13,303
Linux has that and a utility that exists.

131
00:10:13,303 --> 00:10:16,657
The next one we're going to
talk about is called IP route.

132
00:10:16,657 --> 00:10:22,775
And so this is the utility for Linux
forwarding table management, all right?

133
00:10:22,775 --> 00:10:28,257
So you can find the demand page but
you can say much like the rest

134
00:10:28,257 --> 00:10:35,323
of the commands that we're seeing as part
of this IP route two utility package,

135
00:10:35,323 --> 00:10:41,227
it's going to say, IP route or IP and
then some command like route and

136
00:10:41,227 --> 00:10:46,631
then whatever the command is
that we're using the utility.

137
00:10:46,631 --> 00:10:51,623
All right, so here's kind of
snippet from the man page and

138
00:10:51,623 --> 00:10:57,655
if you haven't kind of seen things
like this before, how to read it.

139
00:10:57,655 --> 00:11:00,167
So IP route, that's the command.

140
00:11:00,167 --> 00:11:01,019
It'll be one of these.

141
00:11:01,019 --> 00:11:07,058
So this is an or and
then you specify the route.

142
00:11:07,058 --> 00:11:10,313
So you come down here and
what does a route mean?

143
00:11:10,313 --> 00:11:14,241
A route consists of a node spec and

144
00:11:14,241 --> 00:11:18,740
optionally an info spec, all right?

145
00:11:18,740 --> 00:11:23,414
So the node spec, then you come down here
and say what does the node spec look like?

146
00:11:23,414 --> 00:11:28,913
Well, it'll have all of these, so
key, it'll have a prefix, all right?

147
00:11:28,913 --> 00:11:34,729
And then you can specify other things like
a table and things like that, all right?

148
00:11:34,729 --> 00:11:36,812
So all of that is the node spec.

149
00:11:36,812 --> 00:11:43,815
And then if we look at the info spec,
here, we're seeing similar things.

150
00:11:43,815 --> 00:11:46,381
Here's a next top or an NH.

151
00:11:46,381 --> 00:11:54,318
Come down to NH, it's going to be things
like via some address or dev, some string.

152
00:11:54,318 --> 00:11:56,215
All right, so let's put this all together.

153
00:11:56,215 --> 00:12:01,144
This shown here is a common
one that you'll be seeing,

154
00:12:01,144 --> 00:12:04,474
and we're going to use IP route add.

155
00:12:04,474 --> 00:12:06,252
So the command is add.

156
00:12:06,252 --> 00:12:09,390
So we're adding an entry
to the routing table.

157
00:12:09,390 --> 00:12:12,805
We're going to give it the prefix,
all right?

158
00:12:12,805 --> 00:12:16,548
So that's as part of the node spec.

159
00:12:16,548 --> 00:12:18,457
Here's this prefix, but

160
00:12:18,457 --> 00:12:22,635
we're not specifying any other
options as part of this.

161
00:12:22,635 --> 00:12:26,013
And then as part of that infosec and

162
00:12:26,013 --> 00:12:31,029
the NH we're going to say
the dev string or dev eth3.

163
00:12:31,029 --> 00:12:33,530
And so that's that kind of next top field.

164
00:12:33,530 --> 00:12:38,368
So this is adding an entry
that's saying IP route

165
00:12:38,368 --> 00:12:43,326
add this prefix with
the next top going out eth3.

166
00:12:43,326 --> 00:12:47,745
So we saw there's an option to have a via.

167
00:12:47,745 --> 00:12:52,533
So if you see here,
we could have said dev or

168
00:12:52,533 --> 00:12:56,666
we could have said via some address.

169
00:12:56,666 --> 00:12:58,145
They can include both.

170
00:12:58,145 --> 00:13:01,401
But what does the via do?

171
00:13:01,401 --> 00:13:08,118
So we might have a command like
IP route add all of that via some

172
00:13:08,118 --> 00:13:13,806
address device eth3 or
ip route add device eth3.

173
00:13:13,806 --> 00:13:19,177
So what the via does is
it indicates that there's

174
00:13:19,177 --> 00:13:23,636
a hop in between at the IP layer, right?

175
00:13:23,636 --> 00:13:29,406
So it indicates that the next hop
is not going to be the destination.

176
00:13:29,406 --> 00:13:34,800
And why this is important is
because of what is happening

177
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:40,556
between each of these routers
is there's a layer two here.

178
00:13:40,556 --> 00:13:43,199
So in Ethernet we have MAC addresses.

179
00:13:43,199 --> 00:13:48,815
So when this guy on the left
hand side receives a packet and

180
00:13:48,815 --> 00:13:52,208
is destined to some destination,

181
00:13:52,208 --> 00:13:57,006
let's say 11.11.11.1.1, well,

182
00:13:57,006 --> 00:14:02,037
if it has the via keyword
in it to create this entry,

183
00:14:02,037 --> 00:14:06,483
that means it knows when
it does the next hop,

184
00:14:06,483 --> 00:14:12,216
it should actually not just send
the packet out and look for

185
00:14:12,216 --> 00:14:19,610
the address 11.11.1.1 in the ARP table or
the MAC address table.

186
00:14:19,610 --> 00:14:25,348
Instead, it should look for 192.168.2.1.

187
00:14:25,348 --> 00:14:26,906
And that's what it can set.

188
00:14:26,906 --> 00:14:31,512
It'll set the destination
MAC address of whatever

189
00:14:31,512 --> 00:14:36,553
the next router is with this 192.168.2.1.

190
00:14:36,553 --> 00:14:39,935
So it won't include that IP
address as part of it, but

191
00:14:39,935 --> 00:14:41,823
it will be in the MAC address.

192
00:14:41,823 --> 00:14:47,884
Now in this one because it is directly
connected, then the end host,

193
00:14:47,884 --> 00:14:52,900
that's the destination here
you would not need the via or

194
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:57,079
not use the via because
when it wants to send it,

195
00:14:57,079 --> 00:15:02,542
it wants to send it to
the MAC address of 11.11.1.1.

196
00:15:02,542 --> 00:15:07,056
All right, so that's the difference
between with via and without via.

197
00:15:07,056 --> 00:15:13,613
So we just kind of overviewed this,
mapping the IP to a MAC.

198
00:15:13,613 --> 00:15:18,123
So here X wants to talk to Y on a LAN.

199
00:15:18,123 --> 00:15:23,420
X more likely knows the IP address of Y.

200
00:15:23,420 --> 00:15:26,520
Maybe it's the name of the server and

201
00:15:26,520 --> 00:15:31,125
we've previously covered
DNS in a different course.

202
00:15:31,125 --> 00:15:35,526
But this allows for consistent
communication in a larger network.

203
00:15:35,526 --> 00:15:39,442
But, the link layer communication
is done with MAC addresses.

204
00:15:39,442 --> 00:15:43,451
And so
this is where the ARP protocol comes in.

205
00:15:43,451 --> 00:15:48,201
So as I was saying,
when you have the address to look for

206
00:15:48,201 --> 00:15:53,898
what the next hop is,
you're still sending it out device eth3.

207
00:15:53,898 --> 00:15:57,317
But what are you ARPing for?

208
00:15:57,317 --> 00:16:01,861
You're saying I'm looking for
some IP address, what's its MAC address,

209
00:16:01,861 --> 00:16:02,571
all right?

210
00:16:02,571 --> 00:16:07,722
So it would either be the 192 address and

211
00:16:07,722 --> 00:16:13,157
then get a response for
that so that it can send

212
00:16:13,157 --> 00:16:18,449
the Ethernet frame or
it would ARP at the IP,

213
00:16:18,449 --> 00:16:23,190
the 11.11.1.1, all right?

214
00:16:23,190 --> 00:16:26,906
So that's ARP.

215
00:16:26,906 --> 00:16:33,648
And what's stored is an ARP table or
neighbor table or a MAC address table.

216
00:16:33,648 --> 00:16:38,821
There's different names you can use for
it, but what it's storing

217
00:16:38,821 --> 00:16:44,099
is what the neighbor is and
what its MAC address would be, all right?

218
00:16:44,099 --> 00:16:48,436
And so ARP happens when you
do a lookup in this table,

219
00:16:48,436 --> 00:16:53,867
you don't find the IP address
you're looking for and you do ARP.

220
00:16:53,867 --> 00:17:02,095
All right, so the utility in Linux that
helps with this is ip neigh or neighbor.

221
00:17:02,095 --> 00:17:07,141
And this is the arp table
management utility for Linux.

222
00:17:07,141 --> 00:17:09,421
And here's the link to the man page.

223
00:17:09,421 --> 00:17:13,680
And here's a little snip of some commands.

224
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:14,771
So there's different parts.

225
00:17:14,771 --> 00:17:20,259
So for the most part you probably
don't even need to worry about this.

226
00:17:20,259 --> 00:17:22,338
Like ARP just happens under the table.

227
00:17:22,338 --> 00:17:26,703
But it's useful to know these
because in case there's any sort of

228
00:17:26,703 --> 00:17:31,325
troubleshooting you want to do or
you want to be able to avoid ARP,

229
00:17:31,325 --> 00:17:34,408
maybe you want consistent performance and

230
00:17:34,408 --> 00:17:39,053
not have to do ARP at the beginning
of any kind of traffic stream.

231
00:17:39,053 --> 00:17:44,261
So you can statically add
entries like pseudo IP,

232
00:17:44,261 --> 00:17:48,601
neigh, add whatever the IP address is and

233
00:17:48,601 --> 00:17:53,933
then the link layer address,
the MAC address, and

234
00:17:53,933 --> 00:17:58,895
you can associate it with a device,
all right?

235
00:17:58,895 --> 00:18:05,683
You can show the ARP table or the neighbor
table with the ip neigh show and

236
00:18:05,683 --> 00:18:09,994
it'll show all of those and
you can flush it.

237
00:18:09,994 --> 00:18:13,521
If you want to say ip neigh flush all.

238
00:18:13,521 --> 00:18:16,317
And that basically just
deletes all the entries.

239
00:18:16,317 --> 00:18:21,819
If say something same IP address,
got reassigned

240
00:18:21,819 --> 00:18:27,460
to a new machine then it
would get a new MAC address.

241
00:18:27,460 --> 00:18:31,778
Well, maybe that's a reason
to do the flush so

242
00:18:31,778 --> 00:18:38,113
that it kind of forces this to happen,
those entries to get removed.

243
00:18:38,113 --> 00:18:40,943
And you can remove individual entries.

244
00:18:40,943 --> 00:18:45,516
So this is again mostly for
kind of troubleshooting or

245
00:18:45,516 --> 00:18:50,507
special cases but
it's definitely useful to know about.

246
00:18:50,507 --> 00:18:52,932
All right, so
with that we're going to end here.

247
00:18:52,932 --> 00:18:55,482
Next time we'll actually pull up a demo.

248
00:18:55,482 --> 00:19:01,784
We'll walk through some commands,
we'll see them working in action.

249
00:19:01,784 --> 00:19:04,003
All right, until then,
I'll talk to you then.

250
00:19:04,003 --> 00:19:04,755
Bye.